  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Generally dry weather through early next week. \nDrought conditions will worsen.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nCurrent high pressure ridge continues to shift eastward, \nallowing for stronger return flow out of the SW to advect warmer\nair into the region. Highs on Thursday be quite above average \nfor this time of the year, lifting into the low to mid 60s \nthroughout the forecast area.\n\nAnother cold front will cross the forecast area early Friday\nmorning, and will be mostly moisture starved. There is a chance\nfor scattered rain showers along this boundary, however, \nrainfall amounts are likely to remain below a tenth of an inch, \nwith no improvement to drought conditions. Chance for another \nround of showers, along a shortwave behind the front. \nUnfortunately, another moisture starved system, resulting in \nminimal precipitation. \n\nHigh pressure returns through the weekend, keeping the pattern\ndry. Models are slowing a low pressure system to our west, now\nprojecting primary and secondary FROPA to cross our CWA some \nWednesday into Thursday. PWATs are 0.50-1.25\", along with \nrelatively dry RHs at the surface the precipitation would need \nto overcome. Overall, again, no improvement to drought \nconditions are expected with these showers. \n\nOtherwise, the dry conditons is bad news for the ongoing water\nresources drought impacting the state. Much of Northern Maine, \nCentral Highlands and Eastern Downeast Maine are experiencing \nonly 50-75% of normal precipitation over the last 30 days. While\nin the last 14 days areawide is less than 90% of normal. \nOverall, 30 day Standardized Precipitation Index blend has the \nNorthern 2/3rd of the CWA in the Moderate to Severe Dryness \ncategory, with portions of the Upper St. John Basin now dipping\ninto the Extreme Dryness category. \n\nThere is 38 USGS streamflow gages across the CWA which remain \ndismal, with 26 out of the 38 ranked Much Below Normal (<10th \npercentile). USGS Groundwater sites continue to show struggling \ngroundwater conditions, Clayton Lake is at Record Low (deep) \nconditions on record (35 year POR). Millinocket, Kenduskeag, \nHadley Lakes and Calais all reporting Below to Much Below Normal\nconditions. Fort Kent is very low end of Normal conditions \napproaching slipping down into Below Normal conditions. \nTherefore, the US Drought Monitor continues the entire CWA in at\nleast Moderate Drought with 58% of the CWA in Severe Drought, \nthis has the potential to worsen with cMinimal fog on the waters \nthrough the weekend.ontinued precipitation deficits.",
